  "narrative": "This synthesis reassesses the two response-deadline validations and the new independent validation for the CDKN2A/MDM2 association. All three are concordant and strengthen the supported dataset-specific finding set. H9 is explicitly reassessed using discovery R8 and validation R39. For the registered negative-direction claim that MSI-high cells have stronger WRN dependency, the independent validation is signed positive: estimate +0.8215, interval [0.7661, 0.8768]. Because estimates are already signed, this means the raw MSI-high-minus-non-MSI-high contrast is strongly negative, i.e. much more negative dependency_WRN and therefore much stronger WRN dependency. The magnitude is enormous relative to the WRN dependency mean of about -0.301; even the validation lower bound is far above the 10% clinical-significance threshold. Discovery R8 (+0.7899) and validation R39 are highly concordant, so H9 remains accepted and closed. H20 is reassessed using discovery R24 and validation R40. Among TP53-intact cells, KEAP1 loss versus KEAP1 intact predicts weaker NFE2L2 dependency; validation is signed positive +0.7948, interval [0.7637, 0.8259], meaning the raw KEAP1-loss contrast is strongly positive and less negative, i.e. weaker dependency. This is essentially identical to discovery and far exceeds the 10% threshold. It also aligns with the independently validated TP53-loss stratum H26, making the KEAP1/NFE2L2 association large and robust across TP53 context. H99 is now accepted on independent validation. Among TP53-intact cells, CDKN2A loss versus intact predicts stronger MDM2 dependency; validation is signed positive +0.1162, interval [0.0584, 0.1741]. The raw interpretation is a negative MDM2 dependency difference of about 0.116, i.e. more negative dependency in CDKN2A-loss cells. The validation lower bound is about 22% of the MDM2 dependency mean magnitude, comfortably above the 10% clinical-significance threshold, and discovery R54 (+0.1185) is concordant. I therefore accept H99 and close its investigation as a validated dataset-specific association. Overall supported findings: SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high status is associated with much stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; and CDKN2A loss in TP53-intact cells is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. The first three have independent validation and extensive context checks. The KEAP1/NFE2L2 association is robust across TP53-intact, TP53-loss, STK11-intact, library, and constructed-marker negative-control checks. The CDKN2A/MDM2 result is newly independently validated and mechanistically plausible through p14ARF/CDKN2A biology, although its dependence on MTAP co-deletion remains unresolved. Negative or null findings remain important: constructed marker D/E/F effects and many interactions are near null; ARID1A loss is not associated with SMARCA2 dependency among SMARCA4-intact cells; BRCA2 loss is not associated with WRN dependency among MSI-negative cells; ERBB2 amplification in KRAS-wild-type cells does not show a clinically meaningful effect; high-expression-restricted MYC-amplification effects on DHX9, POLR2A, and RPL11 are not supported; screen-quality, batch, culture, growth-rate, and several co-mutation interactions do not materially overturn the large main effects. The direct driver-marker effects tested so far, including KRAS mutation overall, BRAF, EGFR, ERBB2, and TP53 for MDM2, are generally small or not clinically meaningful in this dataset. Unresolved uncertainty remains for MTAP-independent effects, CDKN2A/MDM2 specificity in the absence of MTAP co-loss, whether CDKN2A effects persist when TP53 is lost, the post-hoc reversed NF1/KRAS observation, USP7 under high MYC expression, and many registered media, Cas9, ploidy, mutation-burden, and driver-context interactions. The most informative next analyses are the newly registered MDM2 specificity/TP53-context tests and the NF1/KRAS adenocarcinoma consistency test, followed by completing robustness interactions for the validated large effects and any already registered unresolved biological hypotheses. All conclusions remain associations within the supplied dataset, not causal effects or externally generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Updated supported validated associations: H7 SMARCA4 loss -> much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H9 MSI-high -> much stronger WRN dependency; H19/H20/H26 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, robust across TP53 strata and many contexts; H99 CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact cells -> stronger MDM2 dependency, discovery signed +0.1185 [0.0793,0.1577] and independent validation signed +0.1162 [0.0584,0.1741], clinically meaningful relative to MDM2 mean. Accepted context claims: H21/H22 SMARCA4/SMARCA2 in adenocarcinoma and ARID1A-intact; H23/H55/H74 MSI/WRN in adenocarcinoma/adherent/Avana; H73/H75/H76/H77 library context KEAP1/NFE2L2 and SMARCA2; H107 KEAP1/NFE2L2 among STK11-intact. Negative/null: original H5 KEAP1 stronger NFE2L2 rejected; marker D/E/F main/interaction null; batch/culture/marker interactions mostly null or small; ARID1A among SMARCA4-intact rejected; BRCA2 among MSI-negative rejected; STK11 co-loss KEAP1 interaction rejected; growth-rate and screen-quality interactions rejected; MYC high-expression effects on DHX9/POLR2A/RPL11 rejected; ERBB2 amp among KRAS-wt rejected. Post-hoc unresolved: H111 reversed NF1 loss among KRAS-wt -> weaker KRAS dependency (raw positive difference ~0.039), interval excludes zero and >10% relative, but remains active/unresolved because registered after seeing H101; new proposal tests consistency in adenocarcinoma. Unresolved main issues: H100 MTAP among TP53-intact MDM2 remains unresolved; USP7 high-MYC interval wide; media-serum/Cas9/ploidy/mutation-burden interactions and remaining driver/main-effect hypotheses untested. New proposals: CDKN2A loss in TP53-loss cells; CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact MTAP-intact to test CDKN2A-specificity; MTAP loss among TP53-intact CDKN2A-intact to test MTAP-independence; NF1 loss among KRAS-wt adenocarcinoma for reversed KRAS observation. Next analyze should prioritize these specificity/context tests and complete registered robustness interactions for validated large effects. All are dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable."
